69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171436 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171436 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171436 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a509034c-c45a-5da3-97a0-63e447121f98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171437 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171437 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171437 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b56004d7-7f40-5724-8ddb-6040508abc14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171438 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171438 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171438 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fb8b1bc6-5bf1-5707-a285-a177c1e41795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171439 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171439 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171439 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2499ca89-587f-582f-b791-58c20a72926d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171440 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171440 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171440 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "335d2d0e-05de-5522-a50e-86e00f91da1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171794 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171794 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n\nII. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n\nIII. The weight of a child at birth" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171794 #edb/ref 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171795 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171795 #edb/ref 1071 "At an electronics store, smartphone purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $20$ minutes. Rounded to four decimal places, what is the probability that the next smartphone is sold within the next $5$ minutes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171795 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/71/714585d89ba3f1d529cd6b64658b778eafa8c346ff9ec592d4997e56803b913e.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda = 1/20$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda = 1/20,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{20} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/9f/9f4bcc34982b48061f0fda3c3136e97f37030cb1cf7d526919860ec53274c644.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{-x/20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/20}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/4} \\\\  & ≈0.2212\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171796 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171796 #edb/ref 1071 "At a clothing store, customers purchase clothes for newborns continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $30$ minutes. What is the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171796 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a82ddd5e5aa9cc641d3720adb9b998c9bc7e72c4e6edfb929d413a6fc584f87.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/79/7960b1171c6697fe49628a8f49ac07671d05acf323ebdfb9734175e12a605640.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 60) & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/30}|}_{60}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171797 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171797 #edb/ref 1071 "A malfunctioning internet server approves connection requests continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ requests per minute. If a user has been waiting for $30$ seconds to connect, what is the probability that they will have to wait at least $20$ more seconds?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171797 #edb/ref 1074 "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}$ requests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/30)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.513.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172005 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172006 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172006 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172006 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172006 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172006 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172006 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172007 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172007 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172007 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172007 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172007 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172007 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172008 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172175 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "deaf4c92-c8a0-5ffb-ac01-faeb66f39a39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172617 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country\n\nII. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\nIII. The number of defective bottles produced in a factory in a single day" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aedbbdaa-f6b9-52ee-98ba-ab79d3a64164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172617 #edb/ref 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Earthquakes can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next earthquake occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective bottles produced is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172619 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172619 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172619 #edb/ref 1071 "At a particular underwater location, earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of once every $2.5$ months. Determine the probability that the next quake will not happen for at least $6$ months. Round your answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7dad992-f274-5c2b-bce5-a1d7040d3be3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172619 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{5}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172635 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172635 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172635 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172635 #edb/ref 1071 "The total time until a computer component registers an error varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of once every $30$ days. Determine the probability that a component will register its next error within the next $2$ to $12$ days. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172635 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb32904c-884e-5312-98bc-b88a7bbf47ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172635 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{30}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172853 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172853 #edb/ref 1061 231085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86f3cc3d-b825-558e-89db-f7ded0fe1e84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172854 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172854 #edb/ref 1061 231086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172854 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8119b3b-4fff-596e-94aa-acf0dc17ccff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172854 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172855 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172855 #edb/ref 1061 231089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172855 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ef50142-79f2-5496-a8b7-805b3150108d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172855 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172856 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172856 #edb/ref 1061 231092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172856 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e0e069a-0219-5cb2-bbfa-c62742dcfbf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172856 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172857 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172857 #edb/ref 1061 231095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172857 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48fbdce6-fb42-59ad-9328-65fe0616ba01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172857 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172858 #edb/ref 1061 231096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a11e0baa-9867-5238-a3a8-1e70022bc1f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173121 #edb/ref 1064 "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda.$ \n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/72/72cd99ec8ff51659357f00417203ace84164027e0cb88584cc136f608d86b15a.svg)\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-3x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]+[1] \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.9502.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173121 #edb/ref 1065 10786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173121 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3c557f1b-8f84-52d6-823a-8025e70b3114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173121 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173122 #edb/ref 1064 "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed. \n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate $\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{ decays/minute}.$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-0.8x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.5507.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1,$ so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big|\\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173122 #edb/ref 1065 10787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173122 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "50a52f5a-aa3a-5935-9354-a35e097ff394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173122 #edb/ref 1067 "Modeling Using the Memoryless Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435478 #edb/ref 1080 "0.982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435478 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435478 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435479 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435479 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435479 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "147b0fac-812b-5ffc-b68e-0bb7f6a18d22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435480 #edb/ref 1080 "0.393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435480 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435480 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435481 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435481 #edb/r